[CG6 the dual — is REFINEMENT closed?]
  Splitting one actor into two cannot be tested by a MAP (a map
  cannot be one-to-many).  The well-posed dual is: can every
  admissible record of a SMALLER actor set be realized as the
  coarse image of some admissible record of a LARGER one?  That is
  the SURJECTIVITY of the coarse-graining, and it is decidable
  here by enumeration.
  admissible COARSE records (2 actors, <= 4 events): 1190
  of these, reachable as the image of an admissible FINE record: 1190
  [PASS] CG6 THE DUAL, REPORTED IN WHICHEVER DIRECTION IT CAME OUT — and it came out the OPPOSITE way to the merge test.  **COARSE-GRAINING IS SURJECTIVE**: every admissible coarse record lifts to some admissible fine record.  (This gate was first written asserting non-surjectivity; it FIRED, and the assertion is withdrawn.  Only the CG1/CG2 controls are exit-1 conditions per pin §5 — a dual that comes out the other way is a deliverable.)  THE CORRECT PICTURE IS THEREFORE ONE-DIRECTIONAL: coarse-graining is a PARTIAL map — undefined on the fine records that break — but ONTO the coarse world it does reach  (coarse records = 1190, reachable = 1190, unreachable = 0 -> SURJECTIVE)

[CG8 the CAUSAL-ORDER version — ROUND-1 D1]
  literal-image failures = 10608; of these, CAUSAL POSET realized by SOME admissible coarse record = 10608
  [PASS] CG8 THE HEADLINE IS NARROWED (ROUND-1 D1).  Of the 10608 records whose LITERAL merged image is inadmissible, 10608 have a causal poset that IS realized by some admissible coarse record — **zero exceptions**.  So actor-merging never produces an UNREALIZABLE CAUSAL SHAPE.  What CG3 established is that the LABELLED record does not aggregate (the mint chain breaks); it establishes nothing directly about the causal order.  The round measured the same at CAP 5 over 196,304 failures, also 100% [REFEREE-CARRIED, frozen round]  (failures = 10608, causal shape realizable = 10608)

[CG9 the real obstruction is LOSS — ROUND-1 D2]
  length 1: FINE (4 actors) causal poset classes = 1, COARSE (2 actors) = 1
  length 2: FINE (4 actors) causal poset classes = 2, COARSE (2 actors) = 2
  length 3: FINE (4 actors) causal poset classes = 4, COARSE (2 actors) = 3
  length 4: FINE (4 actors) causal poset classes = 9, COARSE (2 actors) = 5
  [PASS] CG9 COARSE-GRAINING IS MASSIVELY LOSSY, and this — not impossibility — is the real obstruction.  The fine world has strictly more causal poset classes than the coarse one at length 4 (9 vs 5, a gap of 4), and the round measured the gap WIDENING with length (19 vs 8 at length 5) [REFEREE-CARRIED].  **The coarse description is never impossible; it discards most of the causal structure.**  Any quantity computed after such a projection is a quantity of the COARSE world  (fine classes by length = {1: 1, 2: 2, 3: 4, 4: 9}, coarse = {1: 1, 2: 2, 3: 3, 4: 5})

